  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Management of Accommodation Centres for Ukrainian Refugees Arriving in Ghent, Belgium</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">On 24 February 2022, the Russian Federation launched a broad military offensive against Ukraine. The situation in the country quickly deteriorated, with major attacks being reported across Ukraine, including in the capital, Kyiv. Between 24 February and 25 May 2022, 6.6 million persons have been displaced from Ukraine to neighbouring countries. As of 19 May 2022, on average 350 Ukrainian refugees are being registered in Belgium every day. One third of them are looking for accommodation. Ukrainian refugees who have been registered at Belgian federal level are matched with municipalities in Belgium, where they are hosted by families or in collective accommodation. IOM Belgium and IOM Luxembourg have been selected by Ghent Municipality to manage two accommodation centres in Ghent. IOM aims to manage these centres as protection-sensitive and inclusive spaces, with a strong focus on local integration, building on its years of experience working with national and regional partners.</narrative>
  </description>
